<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Jian (Former Shu)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/847">847</a>–<a href="/years/918">918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Gaozu of Former Shu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Jian was a soldier of humble origin who rose in the Tang army and became military governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 891. After the Tang fell in 907 he proclaimed himself emperor of Former Shu. His wealthy state gave refuge to scholars and poets fleeing the north. His tomb at Chengdu survives with fine stone carvings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-jian-former-shu.jpg" alt="Wang Jian (Former Shu)" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Augusthaiho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%8B%E5%BB%BA%E5%83%8F03430.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Jian, courtesy name Guangtu, came from Wuyang, in modern Luohe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>. He was described as ambitious and alert in youth but also as a hoodlum, who gave up his family's craft of baking for butchering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, stealing donkeys and smuggling salt. Jailed at Xuchang for his misdeeds, he was released in secret by the jailers and stayed for a time with the monk Chuhong in the Wudang Mountains, who urged him to change his ways and foretold great deeds. He then joined the army of Zhongwu Circuit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 881, after the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> general Yang Fuguang formed 8,000 Zhongwu troops into eight corps, one of them under Wang Jian. When Yang died in 883, the corps followed Lu Yanhong in pillaging the region, but in 884 Wang Jian and his friend Han Jian deserted to the eunuch Tian Lingzi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, who adopted them as his sons. During Emperor Xizong's flight from Chang'an in 885, Wang Jian cleared the road through crowds of refugees, bore the imperial seal, and let the emperor sleep on his knees.</p><p>He later took Lang Prefecture and allied with Gu Yanlang of Dongchuan Circuit. Summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 887 and then opposed by its governor Chen Jingxuan, he made war on Xichuan Circuit, whose 12 prefectures his army is said to have pillaged. As military governor of Xichuan he was described as diligent, frugal and open to advice, but also as suspicious, so that many able subordinates were put to death. In 900 Emperor Zhaozong made him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langya-commandery">Langye</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Though illiterate, he liked the company of learned men and kept members of Tang aristocratic families at his court to pass on their knowledge. In 908 he made Lady Zhou empress, and after his adopted son Wang Zongji, a chancellor, challenged him to name an heir, he had Zongji put to death and named his second son Wang Zongyi crown prince. That year his forces joined Qi and Jin in an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, but withdrew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> defeated the Qi army. He died on 11 July 918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Jian (Former Shu)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wang Jian</b>, a soldier from Henan who had served in the palace army, seized Chengdu in 891 and became military governor of the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wuyang County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.44,113.6&amp;year=893" aria-label="Wuyang County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wang Jian (Former Shu)"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4544 4693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4544 4693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.44,113.6&amp;year=893">What was here in 893 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
